WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.820
I recall also in terms of my arrival, my sensing of the atmosphere.

2
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:17.760
I had just been in Europe for fourteen months, and so I saw things maybe sometimes in a European pattern.

3
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:30.840
And I saw Louisiana, at that time, somewhat in the same pattern as a little European principality of some sort.

4
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:37.260
And I know one of the things that gave me that impression: at the time of football games, there was great hullabaloo.

5
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:48.400
Bringing the privileged out to the campus and they came out with blaring horns and police escorts and . . . I don’t know whether there were flags waving,

6
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.853
but it’s the same sense as if they were waving.

7
00:00:50.853 --> 00:00:54.920
And it was all this panoply, all this great, big events.

8
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.460
Football was a big event.

9
00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.200
We used to say that’s when the ladies came out with the fur coats.

10
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:10.660
Imagine coming out with your . . . [laughing] coming out with your . . . You had to have a sense of style to do that in Louisiana.

11
00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:19.180
In those early years, when we won on a Saturday, there was frequently a student demand for a holiday on Monday.

12
00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:23.900
Sometimes it was granted, and I think . . . I think usually, though,

13
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:28.700
if it was granted it was just a half day Monday; no classes Monday morning.

14
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:32.840
It’s the epitome of the opposite of what a university is about.

15
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:36.550
That’s where football really had its effects on academics.
